Yes. Forest bathing is a good example because it is probably not one thing at all. It is a bundle of sensory, attentional, physiological, and contextual inputs that happen to arrive together in a forest.

The evidence is strongest for psychological effects such as reduced stress, anxiety, depressed mood, and improved emotional well-being; physiological effects like blood pressure, cortisol, HRV, and immune markers are promising but more heterogeneous. Recent reviews still emphasize that the exact causal ingredients are not fully separated yet. 

So the Pantheon move would be to factor the bundle:

forest bathing
→ visual complexity without overload
→ natural sound
→ airflow / temperature variation
→ plant odors / volatile compounds
→ walking / gentle movement
→ distance from urban threat/noise
→ soft attention
→ reduced task demand
→ sense of scale / aliveness
→ autonomic downshift

Natural sounds alone appear capable of shifting some stress-related measures relative to quiet conditions, and nature exposure has some evidence for improving working memory, cognitive flexibility, and attention after mental fatigue, though those effects are not uniform. 

The “golden controller” may therefore not be forest specifically. My candidate would be something like:

Restorative Gain Control

perceived safety
+ low forced attention
+ rich but non-demanding sensory variation
+ embodied movement
+ coherent multisensory input
→ sympathetic demand ↓
→ exploratory / restorative mode ↑

Then you can experimentally recreate pieces of it elsewhere.

A room could have:

moving natural light
leaf / water sound
natural textures
subtle plant odor
cool air variation
visual depth
slow movement
no notification pressure

and you could compare it with a real forest and see which components reproduce which deltas.

That gives you the thing you were describing: a sensory controller rather than one fixed environment.

It could have manual channels:

vision 0–10
sound 0–10
scent 0–10
movement 0–10
social input 0–10
novelty 0–10
cognitive demand 0–10
temperature / airflow
light
pace

Then an automatic layer could learn from response:

setting
→ HR / HRV / respiration / movement / self-report
→ observed delta
→ update personal response model
→ suggest or gently adjust next setting

Importantly, you would keep control at the top. Automatic regulation could handle routine things, while you could override or inspect it whenever you want.

That is basically:

subconscious regulation made inspectable.

Humans already do enormous amounts automatically—pupil size, posture shifts, breathing changes, attentional gating, sensory adaptation. The new move is not to replace that system. It is to build an interface around it so some of those normally hidden control variables become visible, adjustable, learnable, and eventually automatic again.

And the experimental version is clean:

real forest = control/reference
→ remove one ingredient
→ measure Δ
→ add it back
→ substitute an artificial version
→ compare Δ
→ repeat across people
→ infer shared vs individual controllers

That would let us distinguish:

forest-specific effects
from
general restorative-state effects
from
person-specific sensory preferences.

That is the golden thread I would chase. Not “trees are magic,” but:

What configuration of sensory input tells a nervous system, reliably enough, that it can stop defending and start restoring?